 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The partitioning step is performed infrequently (only when data changes). The rendering step scales with the number of tetrahedra per process. The compositing step scales with the number of processes and image resolution.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires holding at least two copies of the data during redistribution (original and partitioned) [S1]. The data size grows by an average of 15% per kd-tree level, with variance 5-30% [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Rendering rates increase with the number of processors but show diminishing returns when adding more processors than necessary for a given dataset [S1]. Larger datasets show bigger speedups even if overall frame rates are lower [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Renders unstructured grids natively without sampling into a regular grid. No further cell transfer is required after initial distribution; only image data is communicated [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Data redistribution requires significant memory (two copies). Changes in viewable geometry require lengthy redistribution [S1]. Rendering rates can be impractically low if hardware memory is insufficient [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** Large-scale unstructured grid visualization on distributed-memory parallel computers, especially when native unstructured rendering is preferred over grid sampling.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When memory is constrained and cannot hold two copies of the data. When frequent geometry changes require constant redistribution.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering speed increases with the number of processors but shows diminishing returns when adding more processors than necessary for a given dataset size [S1]. The partitioning time reduces with larger jobs [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires holding at least two copies of the data during redistribution (original and partitioned), which can be a bottleneck if hardware memory is insufficient [S1].
+*   **Data Growth:** Dataset size grows by ~15% on average per kd-tree level, with variance between 5% and 30% [S1]. Larger datasets have smaller relative growth [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Measured on a Dell Precision 530 workstation with dual 2.0 GHz Pentium-4 Xeon processors, 1 GB RDRAM, GeForce FX 5900 Ultra, and Myrinet 2000 interconnect [S1]. Rendering rates were tested on datasets ranging from 44,832 to 14,094,684 tetrahedra [S1]. The system shows bigger speedup for larger datasets even if the overall frame rate is lower [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Renders unstructured grids natively; no further cell transfer after initial distribution; high code reuse from existing volume rendering and compositing codes; works well on distributed-memory parallel computers [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Data redistribution requires significant memory; changes in viewable geometry require lengthy redistribution; rendering rates can be too low for user interaction without level-of-detail techniques [S1].
 
 ## Background
-This method was developed for ParaView and deployed at Sandia National Laboratories for visualizing simulations from supercomputers like Red Storm [S1]. It addresses the challenge of rendering large unstructured grids generated by high-fidelity physical simulations [S1]. The approach leverages the efficiency of the projected tetrahedra method and extends it to a parallel environment using kd-tree partitioning [S1].
+This method was developed for ParaView and deployed at Sandia National Laboratories for visualizing simulations from supercomputers like Red Storm [S1]. It was presented in the paper "Parallel Unstructured Volume Rendering in ParaView" by Kenneth Moreland [S1]. The approach addresses the challenges of rendering large unstructured grids from high-fidelity physical simulations, which are often too large to be handled by traditional single-process or grid-sampling methods [S1].
 
